{"status":"confirmed","reasoning":"For x≠16, multiplying by the conjugate simplifies the quotient to 1/(√x+4). Its limit at 16 is 1/8, current option a, agreeing with the earlier calculation and Math Academy's worked solution. The Incorrect grade followed a dont_know submission with no answer.","confidence":1,"answers":[{"key":"selection","correct_value":"\\frac{1}{8}","correct_option":"a","value_type":"math","wrong_value":"\\frac{1}{4}","correct_keys":[],"wrong_keys":[],"ma_value":"\\frac{1}{8}","ma_basis":"worked_solution","ma_evidence":"\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-4}{x-16} & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-4)(\\sqrt{x}+4)}{(x-16)(\\sqrt{x}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{4}^{2}}{(x-16)(\\sqrt{x}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{x-16}{(x-16)(\\sqrt{x}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}","disagrees":false}]}